#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:2
msgid "Radar mosaics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:3
msgid "*Generate analysis-ready data from the SAR mission with radar mosaics*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:6
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:8
msgid ""
"The SEPAL recipe for radar mosaics allows users to generate analysis-"
"ready data (ARD) from the Sentinel-1 C-Band synthetic aperture radar "
"(SAR) mission, which can be used in subsequent analysis, such as land "
"cover classification."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:10
msgid ""
"Unlike optical sensors, a SAR sensor emits its own microwave signal and "
"records what is scattered back to it (the **backscatter**). It therefore "
"acquires images day and night and through clouds, which makes it "
"particularly valuable in persistently cloudy regions such as the tropics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:12
msgid ""
"SEPAL builds the mosaic from the `Sentinel-1 GRD "
"<https://developers.google.com/earth-"
"engine/datasets/catalog/COPERNICUS_S1_GRD>`__ collection available in "
"Google Earth Engine (GEE). Only scenes acquired in **Interferometric Wide"
" swath** (IW) mode with both **VV** and **VH** polarizations are used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:14
msgid ""
"**VV**: vertically transmitted, vertically received. Sensitive to surface"
" scattering (e.g. bare soil, water, rough surfaces)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:15
msgid ""
"**VH**: vertically transmitted, horizontally received. Sensitive to "
"volume scattering (e.g. vegetation canopies)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:17
msgid ""
"All backscatter values are expressed in decibels (dB). Sentinel-1 data "
"are available from 2014 onward."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:19
msgid "The recipe can produce two kinds of outputs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:21
msgid ""
"**Time scan**: for every pixel, statistics (e.g. median, minimum, "
"maximum, standard deviation) are computed from all the acquisitions "
"within a period. Time scans capture both the typical backscatter and its "
"variation over time, and are well suited to land cover mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:22
msgid ""
"**Point-in-time mosaic**: every pixel comes from the acquisition closest "
"to a target date, giving a snapshot of the surface around that date."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:26
msgid ""
"You won't be able to retrieve the images if your SEPAL and Google Earth "
"Engine (GEE) accounts are not connected. For more information, go to "
":doc:`../setup/gee`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:29
msgid "Start"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:31
msgid ""
"To create a new radar mosaic, go to the :btn:`<fa-solid fa-globe>` "
"**Process** section and select :btn:`<fa-solid fa-plus> Add recipe` "
"(first image below). In the **Create recipe** list that opens, select "
"**Radar mosaic** (second image)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:43
msgid ""
"Once the **Radar mosaic** recipe is selected, SEPAL will display the "
"recipe process in a new tab. The first step is to change the name of the "
"recipe. This name will be used to identify your files and recipes in "
"SEPAL folders. Use the best-suited convention for your needs. Simply "
"double-click the tab (see **1** in the image below) and write a new name."
" It defaults to :code:`Radar_mosaic_<creation date>_<creation time>` "
"(e.g. :code:`Radar_mosaic_2026-10-09_14-18-05`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:47
msgid ""
"The SEPAL team recommends using the following naming convention: "
":code:`<aoi name>_<dates>_<measure>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:54
msgid "Parameters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:56
msgid ""
"When a new recipe opens, the **Area of interest** window is displayed in "
"the lower-right corner (see **2** in the image above). To its right, "
"three tabs are available, which allow you to customize the mosaic "
"creation to your needs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:58
msgid ":guilabel:`AOI`: area of interest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:59
msgid ":guilabel:`DAT`: dates of the mosaic (time scan or point in time)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:60
msgid ":guilabel:`OPT`: processing options (orbits, corrections, filters)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:62
msgid ""
"Select a tab to open the corresponding panel. Each panel is described in "
"the following sections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:65
msgid "AOI selection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:67
msgid ""
"The data exported by the recipe will be generated from within the bounds "
"of the AOI. There are multiple ways to select the AOI in SEPAL:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:69
msgid "Administrative boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:70
msgid "EE Tables"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:71
msgid "Drawn polygons"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:72
msgid "Assets or recipes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:74
msgid ""
"They are extensively described in our documentation. For more "
"information, read :doc:`../feature/aoi_selector`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:77
msgid "Dates"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:79
msgid ""
"In the :guilabel:`DAT` tab, you can define the dates or time frame of the"
" mosaic. There are three ways of specifying the dates; select one of the "
"following options under :guilabel:`Type`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:81
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Year`: builds a **time scan** from all acquisitions of a "
"calendar year (1 January to 31 December). Select the **Year** in the "
"picker. This is the default, and the recommended option for land cover "
"mapping."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:82
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Date range`: builds a **time scan** from all acquisitions "
"between a **From date** and a **To date** of your choice (e.g. a single "
"season, or several years)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:83
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Date`: builds a **point-in-time mosaic**. Select a **Target "
"date**; each pixel will come from the acquisition closest to that date "
"(SEPAL searches up to about six months before and after it)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:91
msgid ""
"The type you choose determines which bands can be exported. Time scans "
"provide statistical bands (e.g. :code:`VV_med`), while point-in-time "
"mosaics provide the backscatter of a single acquisition (e.g. "
":code:`VV`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:94
msgid "Options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:96
msgid ""
"The :guilabel:`OPT` tab controls which acquisitions are used and how they"
" are corrected and filtered. The panel opens in a **simple** view showing"
" the most common options; select :guilabel:`More` to reveal the "
"**advanced** options (and :guilabel:`Less` to hide them again)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:100
msgid "This step is optional. By default, SEPAL applies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:102
msgid "Orbits: **Ascending** and **Descending**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:103
msgid "Geometric correction: **Terrain**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:104
msgid ""
"Spatial speckle filter: **Lee Sigma** (kernel size 9, sigma 0.9, strong "
"scatterers retained)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:105
msgid "Multitemporal speckle filter: **None**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:106
msgid "Outlier removal: **Moderate**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:107
msgid "Orbit overlap: **All**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:108
msgid ""
"Border noise correction: **Sides** and **First/Last** (set in the "
"advanced view)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:109
msgid "Min observations: **1** (set in the advanced view)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst
msgid "Show the advanced view (opened with **More**)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:125
msgid "Orbits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:127
msgid ""
"Sentinel-1 acquires images while travelling north (**Ascending**) and "
"south (**Descending**). The two orbit directions look at the surface from"
" opposite sides, so slopes facing the sensor in one direction face away "
"from it in the other. Select one or both directions (at least one is "
"required)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:129
msgid ""
"Combining both directions increases the number of observations and "
"coverage, but differences in viewing geometry can create visible "
"artefacts, especially in mountainous areas."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:133
msgid "Check the orbit coverage of your AOI before deciding which orbits to use:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:135
msgid "Select only :guilabel:`Ascending` and let the mosaic render."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:136
msgid ""
"Deselect :guilabel:`Ascending`, select :guilabel:`Descending` and let the"
" mosaic render."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:137
msgid ""
"If only one orbit direction covers the entire AOI, use that one. If both "
"do, select both, let the mosaic render and check for artefacts caused by "
"combining them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:140
msgid "Geometric correction"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:142
msgid ""
"The backscatter recorded by the sensor depends on the angle at which the "
"signal hits the ground. Geometric correction reduces this effect, so that"
" values become comparable across the image and between orbits:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:144
msgid ""
":guilabel:`None`: No geometric correction is applied (the backscatter is "
"used as **sigma0**)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:145
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Ellipsoid`: Ellipsoid correction (**gamma0** correction) is "
"applied, normalizing the backscatter by the incidence angle over a smooth"
" Earth model. Topography is not taken into account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:146
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Terrain`: Terrain correction is applied. In addition to the "
"incidence angle, SEPAL uses the `Copernicus GLO-30 "
"<https://developers.google.com/earth-"
"engine/datasets/catalog/COPERNICUS_DEM_GLO30>`__ digital elevation model "
"to compensate for the effect of slopes facing towards or away from the "
"sensor, and masks areas affected by **layover** and **shadow**. "
"Recommended in any area with significant relief."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:149
msgid "Spatial speckle filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:151
msgid ""
"All SAR images contain **speckle**: a grainy, \"salt-and-pepper\" noise "
"caused by the interference of the signals scattered back within each "
"pixel. Spatial speckle filters reduce it by taking neighbouring pixels "
"into account, at the cost of some spatial detail:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:153
msgid ":guilabel:`None`: Doesn't apply any speckle filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:154
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Gamma Map`: A statistical (Bayesian) filter that assumes the "
"backscatter of the surface follows a gamma distribution, and estimates "
"the most likely value of each pixel from its neighbours. It smooths "
"homogeneous areas well while preserving edges and texture."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:155
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Lee`: An adaptive filter based on the mean and variance of the"
" pixels within the kernel. It smooths strongly in homogeneous areas (e.g."
" water, fields) and less around edges and bright features, where the "
"local variance is high."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:156
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Refined Lee`: An improved version of the Lee filter that "
"detects edges and filters along them, using differently oriented windows "
"instead of a square one. It preserves edges and linear features (e.g. "
"roads, field boundaries) better, but can be very slow. It uses a fixed "
"window, so it has no **Kernel size** setting."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:157
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Lee Sigma`: An improved version of the Lee filter that only "
"averages the neighbouring pixels whose values are close to the expected "
"value of the centre pixel (within a range set by **Sigma**). This avoids "
"blurring edges and bright point targets, and gives a good balance between"
" speckle reduction and detail. It is the default."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:158
msgid ""
":guilabel:`SNIC`: Simple Non-Iterative Clustering is used to group pixels"
" into small, homogeneous clusters (superpixels), which filters out "
"speckle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:162
msgid ""
"Time scans combine many acquisitions, which already averages out much of "
"the speckle. Speckle filtering has a stronger effect on point-in-time "
"mosaics, which rely on a single acquisition per pixel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:165
msgid "Spatial speckle filter options (advanced view)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:167
msgid ""
"When a filter other than :guilabel:`None` is selected, a summary of its "
"settings appears below the filter buttons. Select it to open the "
"**Spatial speckle filter options** window. The settings available depend "
"on the filter:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:175
msgid ""
"**Kernel size**: the size, in pixels, of the moving window used by the "
"filter (from 3 to 25; default **9**). Larger windows remove more speckle "
"but blur more detail. Applies to :guilabel:`Gamma Map`, :guilabel:`Lee` "
"and :guilabel:`Lee Sigma`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:176
msgid ""
"**Sigma**: the range of values considered similar to the centre pixel "
"(from 0.5 to 0.95; default **0.9**). Larger values include more "
"neighbouring pixels in the average, giving stronger smoothing. Applies to"
" :guilabel:`Lee Sigma`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:177
msgid ""
"**Seed spacing**: the spacing, in pixels, of the cluster seeds (from 3 to"
" 15; default **5**). Larger values create larger clusters. Applies to "
":guilabel:`SNIC`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:178
msgid ""
"**Compactness**: how compact (square) the clusters are (from 0 to 2; "
"default **0.15**). Larger values create more regular clusters; 0 disables"
" spatial distance weighting. Applies to :guilabel:`SNIC`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:179
msgid ""
"**Strong scatterers**: bright, strong scatterers such as buildings don't "
"tend to include much speckle, and some filters tend to blur them. Choose "
":code:`Retain` (default) to keep their original values, or :code:`Filter`"
" to apply the filter to them as well. Applies to all filters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:180
msgid ""
"**VV threshold** and **VH threshold**: the backscatter values (in dB; "
"defaults **0** and **-5**) above which a pixel is considered a strong "
"scatterer. Shown only when :code:`Retain` is selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:183
msgid "Multitemporal speckle filter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:185
msgid ""
"(Shown only when a spatial speckle filter is selected.) Multitemporal "
"filters reduce speckle further by using a series of acquisitions of the "
"same track, preserving more spatial detail than a spatial filter alone:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:187
msgid ":guilabel:`None`: Doesn't apply any multitemporal speckle filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:188
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Quegan`: First applies the selected spatial filter to each "
"acquisition, then restores the detail lost by that filter using the other"
" acquisitions of the same track. Detail that is consistent over time "
"(e.g. edges, small features) is kept, while random speckle is averaged "
"out. For more information, see `S. Quegan et al. [2000] "
"<https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/842003>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:189
msgid ""
":guilabel:`RABASAR`: (Ratio-based multitemporal SAR despeckling.) "
"Averages the acquisitions of the same track into a nearly speckle-free "
"reference image, then applies the selected spatial filter only to the "
"ratio between each acquisition and that reference. This keeps the spatial"
" detail of the reference while preserving the changes specific to each "
"acquisition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:191
msgid ""
"In the advanced view, select the settings summary below the filter "
"buttons to open the **Multitemporal speckle filter options** window, "
"where you can set the **Number of images** (from 2 to 100; default "
"**10**): how many acquisitions of the same track, taken around the middle"
" of the selected period, are used by the filter. More images reduce "
"speckle further but increase processing time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:195
msgid ""
"Multitemporal filtering is computationally intensive and significantly "
"increases the processing time of the mosaic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:198
msgid "Outlier removal"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:200
msgid ""
"Individual acquisitions can contain unusually high or low values (e.g. "
"caused by heavy rain, flooding or processing artefacts). Outlier removal "
"compares, for each pixel, every acquisition with the median of all "
"acquisitions, and removes those that deviate too much:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:202
msgid ":guilabel:`None`: Doesn't remove outliers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:203
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Moderate`: Removes outliers (values more than **3** standard "
"deviations from the median)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:204
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Aggressive`: Removes more outliers (values more than **2.6** "
"standard deviations from the median)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:208
msgid ""
"Outlier removal relies on the temporal statistics of each pixel, so it is"
" most effective with time scans built from many acquisitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:211
msgid "Orbit overlap"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:213
msgid ""
"Neighbouring Sentinel-1 tracks (identified by their **relative orbit "
"number**) overlap, so some pixels are observed from several tracks with "
"slightly different viewing angles. This option defines which tracks "
"contribute to each pixel:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:215
msgid ":guilabel:`All`: Includes values from all orbit numbers for a pixel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:216
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Dominant`: Only includes values from the dominant orbit number"
" (the one with the most acquisitions) for a pixel. This produces a more "
"consistent viewing geometry, at the cost of fewer observations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:217
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Adjacent`: Includes values from the dominant orbit of both the"
" ascending and the descending orbits. When only one orbit direction is "
"selected, this behaves like :guilabel:`Dominant`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:220
msgid "Border noise correction (advanced view)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:222
msgid ""
"Sentinel-1 scenes can contain low-quality, noisy pixels along their "
"borders. Select one or both corrections:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:224
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Sides`: Masks the sides of slices, by removing pixels whose "
"incidence angle falls outside a given range. When selected, a settings "
"summary appears; select it to open the **Border noise correction "
"options** window and set the **Min angle** and **Max angle** in degrees "
"(defaults: **30.88** and **45.35**)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:225
msgid ""
":guilabel:`First/Last`: Masks the beginning of the first slice and the "
"end of the last slice of an acquisition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:228
msgid "Min observations (advanced view)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:230
msgid ""
"Use the **Min observations** slider (from 1 to 50) to mask pixels that "
"have fewer valid acquisitions than the selected value. Statistics "
"computed from only a few observations are less reliable, so increasing "
"this value removes them from the mosaic (leaving gaps where data are "
"scarce)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:232
msgid ""
"To validate your selection, select the :btn:`<fa-solid fa-check> Apply` "
"button."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:235
msgid "Retrieve"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:239
msgid ""
"You cannot export a recipe as an asset or a :code:`.tif` file without a "
"small computation quota. If you are a new user, see "
":doc:`../setup/resource`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:241
msgid ""
"In the upper-right corner, select the :btn:`<fa-solid fa-cloud-arrow-"
"down>` button to open the **Retrieve** panel, where you can select the "
"exportation parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:248
msgid "Bands to retrieve"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:250
msgid ""
"Select the band(s) to export with the mosaic in **Bands to retrieve**. "
"There is no maximum number of bands, but exporting useless bands will "
"only increase the size and time of the output. The available bands depend"
" on the **Type** selected in the :guilabel:`DAT` tab: a **time scan** or "
"a **point-in-time** mosaic."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:253
msgid "Time scan"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:255
msgid ""
"Available when the type is :guilabel:`Year` or :guilabel:`Date range`. "
"The statistics are computed per pixel from all the acquisitions that "
"remain after filtering:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:257
msgid ":guilabel:`VV_min`, :guilabel:`VH_min`: minimum backscatter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:258
msgid ":guilabel:`VV_mean`, :guilabel:`VH_mean`: mean backscatter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:259
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_med`, :guilabel:`VH_med`: median backscatter. Less "
"sensitive to extreme values than the mean."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:260
msgid ":guilabel:`VV_max`, :guilabel:`VH_max`: maximum backscatter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:261
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_std`, :guilabel:`VH_std`: standard deviation of the "
"backscatter, a measure of how much the surface changes over time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:262
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_cv`, :guilabel:`VH_cv`: coefficient of variation of the "
"backscatter (in dB)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:263
msgid ":guilabel:`orbit`: the most frequent relative orbit number of the pixel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:265
msgid ""
"SEPAL can also fit a harmonic model (a linear trend plus a yearly cycle) "
"to the acquisitions of each pixel. The following bands describe that "
"model:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:267
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_t`, :guilabel:`VH_t`: the slope of the linear trend over "
"time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:268
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_phase`, :guilabel:`VH_phase`: the phase of the yearly "
"cycle, i.e. the time of year when the backscatter peaks."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:269
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_amp`, :guilabel:`VH_amp`: the amplitude of the yearly "
"cycle, i.e. the strength of the seasonal variation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:270
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV_res`, :guilabel:`VH_res`: the residuals of the fit, i.e. "
"how well the model describes the data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:274
msgid ""
"The harmonic model is only computed when at least one of its bands is "
"selected, and it increases the processing time. It requires enough "
"acquisitions spread over the period to give meaningful results; it is "
"best suited to periods of a year or more."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:277
msgid "Point in time"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:279
msgid "Available when the type is :guilabel:`Date`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:281
msgid ""
":guilabel:`VV`, :guilabel:`VH`: the backscatter of the selected "
"acquisition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:282
msgid ""
":guilabel:`ratio_VV_VH`: the difference :code:`VV - VH` in dB (equivalent"
" to the VV/VH ratio in linear units)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:283
msgid ":guilabel:`dayOfYear`: the day of the year of the selected acquisition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:284
msgid ""
":guilabel:`daysFromTarget`: the number of days between the selected "
"acquisition and the target date."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:285
msgid ":guilabel:`orbit`: the relative orbit number of the selected acquisition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:289
msgid ""
"For land cover mapping, the median, minimum, maximum and standard "
"deviation bands of both polarizations (:code:`VV_med`, :code:`VV_min`, "
":code:`VV_max`, :code:`VV_std` and their :code:`VH_` equivalents) of a "
"yearly time scan are a good starting point. They can be combined with "
"optical bands in the :doc:`classification` recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:292
msgid "Scale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:294
msgid ""
"You can set a custom scale for exportation by selecting a value in metres"
" (m). The default is **20 m**. Note that requesting a smaller resolution "
"than the native resolution of the data (10 m pixel spacing for Sentinel-1"
" GRD) will not improve the quality of the output, just its size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:297
msgid "Destination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:299
msgid "Choose a single destination for the export:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:301
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Google Earth Engine asset`: the image is exported to your GEE "
"account as an asset. You can export it either as a single **Image** or as"
" an **Image collection** (tiled, which is better suited to large "
"exports), and set its sharing to **Private** or **Public**. The **Asset "
"ID** field defines where the asset is saved in your GEE account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:302
msgid ""
":guilabel:`Google Drive`: the image is exported to the Google Drive of "
"the connected Google account."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:303
msgid ""
":guilabel:`SEPAL workspace`: the image is written to your SEPAL files in "
":code:`.tif` format. The **Workspace path** defines the destination "
"folder (by default :code:`downloads/<recipe name>`, or "
":code:`downloads/<project name>/<recipe name>` if the recipe belongs to a"
" project), and the **Filename prefix** is added to the name of every "
"exported file (by default, the recipe name)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:307
msgid ""
"The **Google Earth Engine asset** and **Google Drive** destinations are "
"only displayed when a Google account is connected to SEPAL. If they are "
"missing, please refer to `Connect SEPAL to GEE <../setup/gee.html>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:309
msgid ""
"Select :guilabel:`More` to show additional export settings (e.g. **Shard "
"size**, **Tile size**, **CRS** and **CRS transform**). The default values"
" are suitable in most cases; reduce the shard or tile size if the export "
"runs out of memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:311
msgid "Select :guilabel:`Retrieve` to start the export process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:314
msgid "Exportation status"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:316
msgid ""
"Open the **Tasks** tab (lower-left corner, using the :icon:`fa-solid fa-"
"list-check` or :icon:`fa-solid fa-spinner` button, depending on the "
"loading status) to follow the export. Each task shows the recipe type, "
"the destination and the name of the recipe (e.g. a **Radar mosaic** "
"exported to the **SEPAL workspace**), together with its current status "
"(e.g. :code:`Google Earth Engine is exporting`). A running task displays "
"a spinner, and a green check mark appears once it has completed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:318
msgid ""
"You can filter the list with :guilabel:`All`, :guilabel:`Active`, "
":guilabel:`Completed` and :guilabel:`Failed / Canceled`, or find a task "
"by name with the **Search tasks** field. Select :btn:`<fa-solid fa-"
"xmark>` to stop a running task and :btn:`<fa-solid fa-trash>` to remove a"
" task from the list."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:330
msgid ""
"Select a task to open its details: the duration and submission time "
"(**Status**), the recipe type and origin (**Recipe configuration**), the "
"destination, output path and access (**Location**), and the export "
"progress or error message (**Progress**)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:332
msgid ""
"The task can also be monitored using the `GEE task manager "
"<https://code.earthengine.google.com/tasks>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:336
msgid ""
"This operation is running between GEE and SEPAL servers in the "
"background. You can close the SEPAL page without stopping the process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:339
msgid "Access"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:341
msgid ""
"Once the export to the SEPAL workspace is complete, you can access the "
"data in your SEPAL folders. By default, the data are stored in the "
":code:`downloads` folder using the following format:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:356
msgid ""
"Understanding how images are stored in a radar mosaic is only required if"
" you want to manually use them. The SEPAL applications are bound to this "
"tiling system and can digest the information for you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:358
msgid ""
"The data are stored in a folder named after the recipe (see `Start`_). "
"Large images are too big to be exported as a single file, so they are "
"divided into smaller tiles (:code:`.tif` files), which are brought back "
"together by a :code:`<recipe name>.vrt` file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:362
msgid ""
"The full folder, with all its files, is required to read the :code:`.vrt`"
" file."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/radar_mosaic.rst:366
msgid ""
"Now that you have exported the radar mosaic to your SEPAL workspace, it "
"can be downloaded to your computer using `file exchange options "
"<../setup/filezilla.html>`__ or used in other SEPAL workflows."
msgstr ""

